{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.KappaWritheAsymptote","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.KappaWritheAsymptote","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.KappaWrithe.correction_zero_of_eps_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.KappaWrithe.correction_zero_of_eps_eq","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/KappaWritheAsymptote.lean#L54-L61","doc":"**The correction term vanishes when the mirror interface terms coincide** (the `ε → 0` limit).\nIf `e₁ = e₂` (and `1 − e₂ ≠ 0`), then `log[(1 − e₁)/(1 − e₂)] / D = 0`.  Applied to\n`e₁ = ε(N,Z)`, `e₂ = ε(Z,N)`, `D = (N − Z)·ln q₀`, this says the correction in `b_pred` is `0` in\nthe limit, so `b_pred = log_{q₀}(τ_p/τ_n)` — the plateau (the trace ratio), independent of any\ninterface factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.KappaWrithe.epsilon_tendsto_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.KappaWrithe.epsilon_tendsto_zero","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/KappaWritheAsymptote.lean#L44-L52","doc":"**The interface term vanishes geometrically.**  For `|z| < 1`, `ε(z,h; a,b) → 0` as `a → ∞`\n(the strand count `Z` grows).  This is the mechanism behind the `b_pred` plateau: the interface\ncorrection dies at large `A`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.KappaWrithe.epsilonShift","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.KappaWrithe.epsilonShift","kind":"def","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/KappaWritheAsymptote.lean#L40-L42","doc":"The interface term `ε` in shifted index form: `ε(z,h; a,b) = z^a · (z − h/2) · (z − h)^b`,\nwith `a = Z − 1`, `b = N − 1`.  (Grouped `z^a · (…)` so the geometric factor is explicit.) ","signatureChunk":0}]}